REFUTED as a two-programs-per-request defect: the numeric distinctness is real, but the crossing is structurally impossible.

(1) Genuinely two numeric programs — yes. sp feeds `let n_splits_max = (window + sp - 1) / sp;` (crates/memra-engine/src/lib.rs:10486), which changes how the SWA window is cut into partial softmaxes that `fa_decode_combine_rows` then reduces — a different FP combine order, not merely different tiling. The repo asserts it itself: lib.rs:10477-10479 "MUST be one value for ALL widths: a t-keyed probe broke decode-vs-verify combine order (stream 9/128)", and docs/FLAGS.md on the sibling axis MEMRA_FA_SPLIT: "EXACTNESS: split count changes combine FP order — run-spec must re-gate (32 broke 9B self-consistency)". So proven-bit-identical is the wrong status; 24 and 32 are different arithmetic.

(2) Not reachable in serving. There is exactly ONE resolution site, and it is process-wide immutable (lib.rs:10481-10485):
    static SPW: std::sync::OnceLock<usize> = std::sync::OnceLock::new();
    let v = *SPW.get_or_init(|| std::env::var("MEMRA_FA_SPW").ok().and_then(|x| x.parse().ok()).unwrap_or(0));
    if v >= 8 { v } else { FA_SPW_DEFAULT.load(Ordering::Relaxed) }
The env parse is cached once per process by the OnceLock. `grep -rn FA_SPW_DEFAULT crates/` returns exactly 3 hits — the static (lib.rs:425), this load (lib.rs:10484), and ONE writer: crates/memra-engine/src/hybrid.rs:1637, inside the `if cfg.gemma4.is_some()` block of `HybridModel::load_from_source_impl`. In memra-server every model load runs in the startup loop `for (name, path, draft) in &models` (crates/memra-server/src/worker.rs:4082, `loaded.insert` at 4203), and that loop finishes BEFORE `let _ = ready_tx.send(Ok((order.clone(), caps)));` and `health.mark_ready()` (worker.rs:4253-4257) — i.e. before /health/readyz go green and before any request can exist. No lazy/on-demand model load, no unload/reload, no per-request or per-model-switch re-store of the atomic. All eight `fa_decode_rows_w` callers (eager decode, verify rows, dc, seqs in hybrid_forward.rs:7669/7924/8096/8130/8598/8749/8773 plus the drafter at gemma_spec.rs:515) go through that same resolution, so decode and verify inside one request read the same sp by construction. Program A (=24) is not in the code at all; it is an operator env choice — exactly the "one canonical output per FA-split config" class that FLAGS.md classifies FLIP-NEARTIE, not a defect.

(3) Already guarded — structurally rather than by an explicit fence: OnceLock (env read once) + a single load-time-only atomic store + a single read site means no in-flight request can observe two widths, and the uniformity law is documented at the site (lib.rs:10477-10479).

Distinct real finding worth its own ticket (NOT the claimed pair): the gemma4 per-variant defaults at hybrid.rs:1628-1654 (FA_SPW_DEFAULT, FA_SP512_DEFAULT, FUSED_MR1_DEFAULT, FA_VEC_MIN_DEFAULT) are PROCESS globals with last-writer-wins semantics. A multi-model worker configured with both a MoE 26B and a dense gemma 31B serves the first-loaded model for the whole process with the second's widths (32/16 vs 64/32), so the same artifact's near-tie greedy stream depends on MEMRA_MODELS ordering and the 2026-07-12 per-variant sweeps are silently defeated. Constant per request, so no mid-request crossing — a cross-model config-bleed/perf bug. Likewise the FLAGS.md:151 SM-count gap is a per-hardware tuning gap (per-hardware-arm doctrine, task #121), not a numeric-program crossing.
